            <ArticleTitle>[Analysis of 12 patients with novel mutations of Dystrophin gene].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>802-805</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3760/cma.j.issn.1003-9406.2017.06.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the characteristics, location, and amino acid changes of novel mutations of the Dystrophin gene.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twelve patients in whom no deletion or duplication of the Dystrophin gene was detected were analyzed with next-generation sequencing. Fifty healthy adult males were recruited as the controls.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All patients were detected with mutations of the Dystrophin gene, which included c.33C&gt;G, c.583C&gt;T, c.1333C&gt;T, c.2593C&gt;T, c.5731A&gt;T, c.7288G&gt;T, c.2803+1G&gt;T, c.10034G&gt;A, c.4289A&gt;G, c.1905_906delAG, c.5017delC, c.5768_5771delAAGA, and c.6261_6262insA. No similar mutations were found among the controls.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our data has enriched the mutation spectrum of the Dystrophin gene and may provide an important basis for genetic diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
